{"w14:TF":{"cols":["kind","quote","section"],"rows":[["design","Here, we used a large panel of 1008 segregants from a cross between a laboratory strain BY4724 (hereafter referred to as BY) and a vineyard strain RM11-1a (hereafter RM) to study yeast growth in haloperidol.","Introduction"],["design","We carried out linkage analysis in a panel of 1008 BY-RM segregants [15] for growth at five different concentrations of haloperidol (40, 80, 120, 160, 200 µM) and identified nine distinct significant QTL ( Fig. 3A ).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["direction","At the major locus on the right arm of chromosome I, the allele from RM (the sensitive parent) promoted growth in the presence of haloperidol, consistent with our observation of transgressive segregation ( Fig. 2B ).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["direction","RM alleles at loci on chromosomes V, XII and XV also confer greater resistance, while BY alleles confer higher resistance at the remaining five loci ( Fig. 3 ).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["interval","Significant loci are shown through their 1.5-LOD drop confidence intervals (blue rectangles).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["effect","Positive values represent better growth in the presence of the BY allele. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004894.g003 Some of the loci were detected only at certain doses of haloperidol, and the effect sizes of most loci were dose-dependent ( Fig. 3 ).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["dose","For instance, the loci on chromosomes VII, XII, and XIII were only detected at the two lower doses, while the effects of loci on chromosomes XIV and XV primarily manifested at the higher doses ( Fig. 3B ).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["ve","This model explained between 35.9% and 54.7% of the total phenotypic variance at concentrations 40–160 µM ( S1 Table ).","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["ve","The locus on chromosome I (right arm) alone explained 21.4%, 26.7%, 21.4%, 11.6% of the variance at these four doses, respectively.","Loci underlying haloperidol resistance have dose-dependent effects"],["candidate","The confidence interval of this peak contained the gene SWH1 (also known as OSH1 ), which encodes a yeast homologue of the mammalian oxysterol-binding protein (OSBP) [29] .","Polymorphisms in the OBP domain of SWH1 underlie major QTL on chromosome I"],["candidate","Thus, SWH1 RM is the resistant allele relative to SWH1 BY , confirming the QTL results.","Polymorphisms in the OBP domain of SWH1 underlie major QTL on chromosome I"],["candidate","Introducing the functional BY allele into the RM background slightly reduced resistance to haloperidol, whereas having the RM allele of SWH1 in BY increased resistance.","Polymorphisms in the OBP domain of SWH1 underlie major QTL on chromosome I"],["candidate","IRA2 , a gene previously identified to contain variants underlying differences in gene expression and metabolite levels [33] , [34] , resides within the locus on chromosome XV.","Polymorphisms in IRA2 and MKT1 contribute to haloperidol resistance"],["candidate","The IRA2 RM allele in both BY and RM genetic backgrounds was more resistant to the drug ( Fig. 5A ).","Polymorphisms in IRA2 and MKT1 contribute to haloperidol resistance"],["candidate","Using BY and RM allele replacement strains from [41] , we confirmed MKT1 as the gene underlying the chromosome XIV locus ( Fig. 5B ).","Polymorphisms in IRA2 and MKT1 contribute to haloperidol resistance"],["candidate","The observation that the BY allele of MKT1 led to greater growth in haloperidol is the opposite of what has been seen for growth in other conditions in previous complex trait studies, which found the MKT1 BY allele to be deleterious for growth in the absence of functional mitochondria and in the presence of the drug 4-NQO [9] , [41] .","Polymorphisms in IRA2 and MKT1 contribute to haloperidol resistance"],["interaction","We tested for statistical interactions between additive QTL detected in at least one haloperidol concentration, and found 9, 10, 3, 5 and 5 significant pair-wise QTL interactions (out of 36 possible locus pairs) at the five doses (Bonferroni-corrected p<0.005, S1 Table , S3 Table ).","Complex genetic interactions among haloperidol resistance loci"],["interaction","Incorporating the corresponding significant two-way QTL interactions in the QTL model at each dose explained an additional 11.0%, 6.6%, 1.7%, 7.8%, and 7.2% of phenotypic variance at 40 µM, 80 µM, 120 µM, 160 µM, and 200 µM haloperidol, respectively ( S3 Table ).","Complex genetic interactions among haloperidol resistance loci"],["method","Linkage was determined by calculating LOD scores for each genotype marker using both Haley-Knott regression and non-parametric linkage mapping with the R/qtl package.","QTL mapping"],["method","QTL were called at a LOD cutoff of 3.","QTL mapping"],["method","Residuals were used for QTL mapping.","QTL mapping"],["data","All relevant data are within the paper and its Supporting Information files.","Author Summary"]]},"w14:S1":{"cols":["file","table","row","heading","c1","c2","c3","c4","c5","c6","c7","c8"],"rows":[["journal.pgen.1004894.s002.docx","1","1","40 μM haloperidol:","","df","Type III SS","LOD","%var","F value","Pvalue(Chi2)","Pvalue(F)"],["journal.pgen.1004894.s002.docx","1","2","40 μM haloperidol:","1L","1","1047.8","17.654","4.2752","83.95","0.000","< 2e-16"],["journal.pgen.1004894.s002.docx","1","3","40 μM haloperidol:","1R","1","5231.9","76.554","21.3477","419.18","0.000","< 2e-16"],["journal.pgen.1004894.s002.docx","1","4","40 μM haloperidol:","2","1","174.5","3.043","0.7121","13.98","0.000","0.000195"],["journal.pgen.1004894.s002.docx","1","5","40 μM haloperidol:","7","1","1199.2","20.092","4.8